{
  "requirement": "`kind` classifies the construct and `span` locates it under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`. Language providers can\nconstruct an attachment from these two fields.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OutlineAttachment {\n    pub kind: AttachmentKind,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\nimpl OutlineAttachment {\n    pub fn new(kind: AttachmentKind, span: Span) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OutlineAttachment;\nimpl Debug for OutlineAttachment;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations from `requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineAttachment`**\n- `OutlineAttachment` is a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with public `kind: AttachmentKind` and `span: Span` fields.\n- `OutlineAttachment::new(kind, span)` constructs an attachment from those two fields.\n- `Clone` and `Debug` are implemented.\n- `span` is governed by `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`. That requirement is cited, so I only checked that the tests apply it in this case.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Constructor and fields:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` has a custom `OutlineLang` provider. Its `outline` builds attachments with `OutlineAttachment::new(AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into()), *span)`. The test then reads the result back:\n  - `attachment.kind == AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")` shows `new` preserves `kind`.\n  - `&snapshot.text()[attachment.span] == \"é\"` shows `new` preserves `span`.\n- **Span semantics:** the same test applies the cited span requirement. Slicing the original snapshot at the span yields the construct. Spans that split `é`, fall outside the snapshot, extend past the item, or are out of order are rejected. A zero-width span after a prior one is accepted.\n- **Other constructor uses:** `outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments` in the same file also builds attachments with `new`. It is extra evidence only, and I did not need it for the verdict.\n- **Declaration shape:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` runs `check_declaration` for each documented item. Against the requirement's published listing and the real rustdoc output, it checks:\n  - the struct declaration and its public fields;\n  - that `#[non_exhaustive]` is present on any struct with public fields;\n  - the `new` signature;\n  - the exact set of trait implementations (`Clone`, `Debug`).\n  It also compiles the Habeas consumer against the SDK.\n\n**Exemptions and gaps**\n- `Clone` and `Debug` are derive-like trait impls with no conditional logic of their own. They are checked by the exact trait-impl inventory, so no behavioral test is needed.\n- I found no unmarked relevant test and no missing scenario.",
